  },
  "teaching_objectives": {
    "knowledge_and_skill_objectives": [
      "Master the definition of chemical equilibrium state, clarify the formation conditions of chemical equilibrium for reversible reactions",
      "Grasp the core characteristics of chemical equilibrium, master the basic method to judge whether a reversible reaction reaches equilibrium",
      "Correct the common misconception that "equilibrium is a static reaction stop state""
    ],
    "process_and_method_objectives": [
      "Through analog inquiry activities, cultivate students' ability of analogical reasoning and scientific thinking",
      "Through group discussion and problem analysis, improve students' ability to apply concepts to solve practical chemical problems"
    ],
    "emotional_attitude_objectives": [
      "Recognize the dynamic nature of chemical changes, establish the scientific view of dynamic equilibrium",
      "Connect equilibrium phenomena in daily life (saturated solution, natural carbon cycle) to feel the universality of equilibrium, stimulate interest in chemical research"
    ]
  },
  "key_and_difficult_points": {
    "key_points": [
      "Concept and formation conditions of chemical equilibrium",
      "Core characteristics of chemical equilibrium state",
      "Judgment criteria for chemical equilibrium"
    ],
    "difficult_points": [
      "Understanding the dynamic nature of chemical equilibrium (v_forward = v_reverse ≠ 0 at equilibrium)",
      "Flexible application of equilibrium judgment criteria to different reversible reaction systems"
    ]
  },
  "classroom_activity_design": [
    {
      "activity_stage": "Introduction (5 minutes)",
      "activity_content": "Teacher raises a daily question: 'When we dissolve sugar in water, after the solution becomes saturated, the mass of undissolved sugar does not change. Does the dissolution process stop completely?' Guide students to recall the concept of reversible processes and reversible chemical reactions learned in the previous lesson, and naturally lead to the core topic of this class: the equilibrium state of reversible reactions."
    },
    {
      "activity_stage": "Inquiry Activity: Analog Simulation of Equilibrium Formation (15 minutes)",
      "activity_content": "1. Divide students into groups of 2, each group is equipped with 2 same-sized conical funnels and 100mL colored water. Rule: Student A holds the funnel representing reactants (initial 50mL water), Student B holds the funnel representing products (initial 0mL water), both transfer water to the other's funnel with the same size spoon at the same time each round. 2. After 5-6 rounds of transfer, students observe that the water volume in both funnels remains basically unchanged, even though they are still transferring water. 3. Group guided discussion: What do the forward and reverse transfer rates correspond to in a reversible reaction? Why does the water volume keep constant? Lead students to conclude independently: when the forward reaction rate equals the reverse reaction rate, the concentration of each substance no longer changes, and the reaction reaches equilibrium, which is a dynamic not static process."
    },
    {
      "activity_stage": "Concept Sorting and Misconception Correction (15 minutes)",
      "activity_content": "1. Teacher formalizes the definition of chemical equilibrium: Under certain conditions, for a reversible reaction, when the rate of the forward reaction equals the rate of the reverse reaction, the concentration, mass fraction of each substance no longer changes, and the system reaches chemical equilibrium. 2. Guide students to summarize the core characteristics of equilibrium, summarized as 5 easy-to-remember keywords: ① Reverse (only occurs in reversible reactions), ② Equal (v_forward = v_reverse), ③ Dynamic (v_forward = v_reverse ≠ 0, reaction does not stop), ④ Constant (concentration and other variables remain constant), ⑤ Change (equilibrium will shift when external conditions change). 3. Interactive misconception judgment: Teacher throws 2 wrong common statements, let students judge and correct: ① 'Chemical equilibrium is a static state, the reaction has stopped', ② 'At equilibrium, the concentration of reactants must equal the concentration of products'. Students speak freely, teacher reinforces the core of dynamic equilibrium."
    },
    {
      "activity_stage": "Consolidation Practice and Sharing (7 minutes)",
      "activity_content": "Give the classic reaction example: For the constant-temperature closed system reaction N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), ask groups to discuss: Which of the following statements can prove that the reaction reaches equilibrium? ① The concentration of N₂ no longer changes, ② v(N₂)_{forward} = v(H₂)_{reverse}, ③ The total pressure of the system no longer changes, ④ The mass fraction of NH₃ no longer changes. Invite 2 groups to share answers, teacher explains wrong options and sorts out the general judgment criteria."
    },
    {
      "activity_stage": "Summary and Homework (3 minutes)",
      "activity_content": "Teacher sorts out the core content of the class, emphasizes that chemical equilibrium is dynamic equilibrium. Assign homework: Complete 5 post-textbook exercises, and extended thinking question: 'According to the 'change' characteristic of equilibrium, guess what will happen to equilibrium if we change the external temperature? Preparate for the next lesson on equilibrium movement.'"
    }
  ],
  "blackboard_design": {
    "main_title": "Chemical Equilibrium",
    "left_area": "1. Definition\nReversible reaction, v_forward = v_reverse, concentration of each substance unchanged",
    "middle_area": "2. Core characteristics\nReverse · Equal · Dynamic · Constant · Change",
    "right_area": "3. Equilibrium judgment\n① v_forward = v_reverse ≠ 0\n② Relevant variables remain constant"
  }
}

