      "Can identify 7 major lithospheric plates on a world map, distinguish three types of plate boundaries (divergent, convergent, transform) and their associated geological features",
      "Can use plate tectonics to explain common geological phenomena such as earthquakes, volcanic eruptions, and mountain formation"
    ],
    "process_and_method": [
      "Improve map reading ability and cooperative inquiry ability through group puzzle and map marking activities",
      "Cultivate logical thinking by sorting out the evolution of the theory and inferring the causes of natural phenomena"
    ],
    "emotional_attitude_values": [
      "Understand that scientific theories are continuously revised and improved with new evidence, forming a rigorous scientific attitude",
      "Recognize the close connection between plate movement and human life, stimulate curiosity about Earth science research"
    ]
  },
  "key_and_difficult_points": {
    "key_points": [
      "Core viewpoints of plate tectonics theory",
      "Types of plate boundaries and corresponding geological features"
    ],
    "difficult_points": [
      "Understanding the driving mechanism of plate movement (mantle convection)",
      "Flexibly applying plate tectonics to explain the formation of different geological phenomena"
    ]
  },
  "teaching_preparation": [
    "Pre-cut continental outline puzzle pieces for each group",
    "Printed blank world tectonic base maps, colored markers",
    "Animation of mantle convection and plate movement, multimedia courseware"
  ],
  "classroom_activity_design": [
    {
      "stage": "Warm-up Lead-in (5 minutes)",
      "activity_content": "1. Divide students into groups of 4, distribute pre-cut continental puzzle pieces, ask groups to splice the pieces into a complete whole according to contour. 2. Raise guiding questions: \"Why do the edges of South America and Africa fit almost perfectly like a broken puzzle?\" 3. Introduce Wegener's continental drift hypothesis, point out that the hypothesis could not explain the driving force of continental movement, leading to the topic of plate tectonics."
    },
    {
      "stage": "Theory Development Inquiry (10 minutes)",
      "activity_content": "1. Briefly introduce the seafloor spreading theory: new evidence from mid-ocean ridge magnetic anomaly detection filled the gap of driving force for continental movement. 2. Explain the core viewpoint of plate tectonics: the lithosphere (crust + upper mantle) is broken into multiple rigid plates, which move slowly driven by the convection of the underlying soft asthenosphere. 3. Play a 2-minute animation of mantle convection to intuitively show the driving mechanism of plate movement, breaking through the first difficult point."
    },
    {
      "stage": "Group Exploration: Tectonic Map Marking (15 minutes)",
      "activity_content": "1. Distribute blank world base maps and colored markers to each group, assign tasks: (1) Label the 7 major lithospheric plates; (2) Mark 3 boundary types with different colors (red for divergent, blue for convergent, yellow for transform) and note typical geological features for each type. 2. After 10 minutes of group cooperation, invite 3 groups to present the characteristics of one boundary type each. 3. Teacher summarizes and corrects: Divergent boundaries form mid-ocean ridges or continental rift valleys (e.g., East African Rift); convergent boundaries form trenches, volcanic mountains or folded mountains (e.g., Himalayas formed by the collision of Indian and Eurasian plates); transform boundaries form large strike-slip faults (e.g., San Andreas Fault)."
    },
