  "Key Points": [
    "Concept of concurrency and its essential difference from parallelism",
    "Core motivations of concurrency in OS: overlapping I/O and computation, handling multiple user requests, leveraging multi-core hardware",
    "Core problems brought by concurrency: race condition and critical section"
  ],
  "Difficulties": [
    "Correctly distinguishing concurrency and parallelism (easily confused by beginners)",
    "Understanding how interleaved instruction execution leads to non-intuitive race conditions",
    "Identifying implicit shared resources that cause hidden concurrency bugs in real systems"
  ],
  "Classroom Activity Design": [
    {
      "Activity Name": "Warm-up: Shared Resource Scenario Discussion",
      "Duration": "10 minutes",
      "Procedure": "1. Teacher raises a life-oriented question: 'If you and your classmate send 5-page assignments to the same shared printer at almost the same time, what bad result may happen if the printer has no management rules?' 2. Students think independently for 2 minutes, then discuss in pairs for 3 minutes. 3. Invite 2 pairs to share conclusions. 4. Teacher summarizes: the problem of multiple interleaved tasks accessing shared resources is exactly the concurrency problem we will learn today.",
      "Activity Objective": "Activate prior knowledge, lead in the concept of concurrency intuitively"
    },
    {
      "Activity Name": "Concept Sorting: Distinguish Concurrency vs Parallelism",
      "Duration": "25 minutes",
      "Procedure": "1. After briefly introducing the two concepts, teacher gives 4 different task execution scenarios: (1) Single-core CPU switching between a document editor and a music player; (2) 4-core CPU running a video conference and a 3D game at the same physical time; (3) Single-threaded web server handling 10 user requests interleaved; (4) Parallel matrix multiplication running on 8 GPU cores. 2. Students work in groups to sort scenarios into 'Concurrency only' and 'Both concurrency and parallelism' within 10 minutes. 3. Teacher checks answers and clarifies: Concurrency means multiple tasks make progress over overlapping time intervals (can be interleaved on one core), parallelism means multiple tasks execute at the exact same physical time. All parallel scenarios are concurrent, but not all concurrent scenarios are parallel.",
      "Activity Objective": "Solve the common confusion between two core concepts, master the essence of concurrency"
    },
    {
      "Activity Name": "Hands-on Simulation: Reproduce Race Condition",
      "Duration": "30 minutes",
      "Procedure": "1. Teacher gives a simple case: shared variable `counter = 0`, two threads A and B each execute `counter = counter + 1` once (the operation is split into 3 CPU instructions: ① Load counter from memory to register ② Add 1 to register ③ Store register value back to memory). 2. Groups of 3 students, role assignment: one is OS scheduler, one is thread A, one is thread B. Students try different scheduling orders to calculate the final value of counter. 3. Invite groups to show results: most groups will get both result 1 and result 2, leading to the conclusion that the result is non-deterministic under interleaved execution. 4. Teacher guides students to summarize the cause: interleaved execution in the critical section (the code segment accessing shared resources) causes race condition.",
