      "implementation_process": [
        "Invite 2 student volunteers to the front, explain game rules: Each player chooses 'Split' or 'Steal'. If both Split, each gets 5 extra credit points; if one Split one Steal, the Stealer gets 10 points and the Splitter gets 0; if both Steal, both get 0",
        "Let volunteers choose secretly, then reveal results and announce outcomes",
        "Ask the whole class guiding questions: 'Why did the players make that choice?' 'Would you change your choice if you knew the other player's choice in advance?'",
        "Summarize: When personal outcome depends on others' choices, it is a strategic interaction (the core research object of game theory), and we need Nash Equilibrium to judge stable interactive outcomes"
      ]
    },
    {
      "activity_name": "Core Inquiry: Prisoner's Dilemma Role-play & Nash Equilibrium Derivation",
      "duration": "18 minutes",
      "activity_objective": "Master the definition and core properties of Nash Equilibrium",
      "implementation_process": [
        "Divide all students into pairs, each pair plays two suspects accused of joint crime, explain payoff rules: If both confess, each gets 5 years in prison; if both stay silent, each gets 1 year; if one confesses one stays silent, the confessor is released and the silent one gets 10 years",
        "Ask each student to write down their choice privately, then count and announce the overall result (most pairs will end up with both confessing)",
        "Draw the payoff matrix on the blackboard, guide students to analyze best response: No matter what the other player chooses, confessing always brings a better personal outcome, so confessing is a dominant strategy",
        "Introduce the formal definition of Nash Equilibrium: A strategy combination where no player can improve their own payoff by changing strategy unilaterally, given other players' strategies",
        "Verify the outcome: When both confess, no player wants to change their choice, so this is a Nash Equilibrium, and dominant strategy equilibrium is a special case of Nash Equilibrium"
