      "Establish a rational understanding of the relationship between market mechanisms and government intervention, cultivate the literacy of connecting theoretical analysis to real economic problems"
    ]
  },
  "key_points": [
    "Contrast and connection between partial equilibrium and general equilibrium analysis",
    "Pareto efficiency criterion and Pareto efficiency conditions under general equilibrium",
    "Core content and policy implications of the two fundamental theorems of welfare economics"
  ],
  "difficult_points": [
    "Intuitive understanding of the existence of general equilibrium and the logic of Walras' Law",
    "Derivation and interpretation of Pareto efficient allocation using the Edgeworth box",
    "Distinguishing Pareto efficiency from equity, clarifying the premise and limitations of the fundamental welfare theorems"
  ],
  "classroom_activity_design": [
    {
      "activity_name": "Warm-up: Minimum Wage Hike Thinking Question",
      "duration": "10 minutes",
      "procedure": "1. Instructor raises the question: If the government raises the minimum wage for the fast-food industry, what change will happen to employment in this industry? Ask 2-3 students to share their answers first. 2. Guide students to extend thinking: What additional impacts will this policy bring to other industries and the whole economy? For example, fast-food prices will rise, pushing up the cost of living for all households; unemployed fast-food workers flow to other industries, changing wages and prices of other goods. 3. Instructor introduces: Partial equilibrium only focuses on the equilibrium of a single market, while general equilibrium considers the mutual influence of all markets, which is the core content of today's class."
    },
    {
      "activity_name": "Group Practice: Edgeworth Box for Pareto Efficiency",
      "duration": "18 minutes",
      "procedure": "1. Divide students into groups of 4, distribute worksheets with the problem: Pure exchange economy with 2 consumers (A, B) and 2 goods (X, Y), total endowment is 10X and 10Y, initial endowment: A(8,2), B(2,8), utility functions: Uₐ = XₐYₐ, Uᵦ = Xᵦ + 2Yᵦ. 2. Assign 3 tasks: draw the Edgeworth box, mark the initial endowment, find the contract curve (all Pareto efficient allocations), list 2 Pareto improving reallocations from the initial endowment. 3. Invite 2 groups to show their results, correct common mistakes (e.g. wrong axis orientation, confusing Pareto improvement with Pareto efficiency), and summarize that the core condition for Pareto efficiency in exchange is equal MRS across consumers."
    },
    {
      "activity_name": "Open Discussion: Do We Still Need Government Intervention If Competitive Markets Are Pareto Efficient?",
      "duration": "12 minutes",
      "procedure": "1. Groups keep their teams, discuss the question for 8 minutes, the instructor reminds students to think about the assumptions of the first fundamental welfare theorem and the difference between efficiency and equity. 2. Invite 2 teams to share their views, then the instructor summarizes: The fundamental welfare theorem only guarantees efficiency under strict assumptions (perfect competition, no externalities, full information, no public goods); when assumptions fail, markets will have inefficiency, requiring government regulation. Even if the market is efficient, the resulting allocation may be extremely unequal, so government redistribution is needed to improve equity."
    },
    {
      "activity_name": "Wrap-up and Exit Check",
      "duration": "5 minutes",
      "procedure": "Summarize the core logic of this class: General equilibrium studies the interdependence of all markets, welfare economics evaluates market outcomes with the Pareto criterion, and the fundamental welfare theorems clarify the efficiency property of competitive markets. Assign an exit question for students to write down: What is one key difference between general equilibrium and partial equilibrium analysis, collect to check mastery."
    }
  ],
  "after_class_arrangement": [
    "Textbook exercise: Prove that any competitive equilibrium in a pure exchange economy is Pareto efficient",
    "Extended thinking: Read the excerpt about income distribution and market efficiency, write a 200-word short answer to the question: Can a Pareto efficient allocation be unfair?"
  ]
}

