    "course_title": "Molecular Oncology",
    "target_audience": "Upper-division undergraduate students / First-year graduate students in Biomedical Science, Biology, Pre-medicine",
    "class_duration": "90 minutes"
  },
  "teaching_objectives": {
    "knowledge_objectives": [
      "Master the definition and core components (cellular components: cancer-associated fibroblasts, tumor-associated immune cells, endothelial cells; non-cellular components: extracellular matrix, cytokines, growth factors) of the tumor microenvironment (TME)",
      "Clarify the multiple mechanisms by which TME promotes cancer progression, including proliferation sustainment, invasion and metastasis, immune evasion, and therapeutic resistance",
      "Understand the translational value of TME research in cancer treatment development"
    ],
    "ability_objectives": [
      "Ability to analyze the bidirectional crosstalk between TME components and cancer cells",
      "Ability to connect basic TME knowledge to clinical phenomena such as immunotherapy resistance and tumor recurrence",
      "Ability to collaborate to explore scientific questions related to TME function"
    ],
    "quality_objectives": [
      "Cultivate systematic biological thinking that tumor progression is a co-evolution process of cancer cells and their microenvironment, rather than a single-driver event of cancer cells",
      "Inspire interest in translational oncology research and the development of targeted anti-cancer therapies",
      "Establish the awareness of personalized cancer treatment based on TME heterogeneity"
    ]
  },
  "key_points": [
    "Composition and structural characteristics of TME",
    "Core mechanisms of TME in promoting cancer progression (proliferation, invasion, metastasis, immune escape)",
    "Clinical translational significance of TME-targeted therapy"
  ],
  "difficult_points": [
    "Understanding the dynamic heterogeneity of TME and the bidirectional crosstalk network between TME components and cancer cells",
    "Connecting TME heterogeneity to the molecular mechanism of clinical cancer therapeutic resistance"
  ],
  "classroom_activity_design": [
    {
      "activity_name": "Warm-up: Clinical Case Divergent Thinking",
      "duration": "15 minutes",
      "procedure": "1. Instructor presents a real clinical case: Two patients with stage II, ER-positive breast cancer with identical cancer cell somatic mutations show completely different outcomes: one achieves complete remission after immunotherapy combined with chemotherapy, the other develops rapid metastasis within 1 year. 2. Divide students into 4-person groups, discuss for 5 minutes: What do you think causes the different outcomes? 3. Invite 2-3 groups to share their opinions, then instructor leads to the topic: The difference comes from the tumor microenvironment, not just cancer cells themselves.",
      "activity_objective": "Activate prior knowledge of cancer genetics, stimulate learning interest, introduce the core status of TME in cancer progression"
    },
    {
      "activity_name": "Group Concept Mapping: TME Component Function",
      "duration": "25 minutes",
      "procedure": "1. After instructor explains the basic composition of TME, assign one TME component to each group: (1) Cancer-associated fibroblasts (CAFs), (2) Tumor-associated macrophages (TAMs), (3) Extracellular matrix (ECM), (4) Tumor-infiltrating T cells. 2. Each group has 10 minutes to sort out and draw a concept map showing how their assigned component interacts with cancer cells to promote cancer progression. 3. Each group presents for 3 minutes, then instructor supplements, corrects wrong understandings, and summarizes the common crosstalk mechanisms.",
      "activity_objective": "Help students systematically sort out the function of different TME components, deepen understanding of their role in cancer progression"
    },
    {
      "activity_name": "Mini Debate: TME Targeting vs Cancer Cell Targeting",
      "duration": "30 minutes",
      "procedure": "1. Divide the class into two groups: Pro side argues 'Targeting TME is a more promising strategy than targeting cancer cells alone for cancer treatment', Con side argues against. 2. 10 minutes of preparation time, 18 minutes of debate (each side has 9 minutes for statements and cross-examination), 2 minutes of conclusion for each side. 3. Instructor makes a summary: Neither strategy is sufficient alone, the core insight of TME research is that cancer progression is a systemic disease of the tumor ecosystem, and combination targeting of cancer cells and TME is the future direction.",
      "activity_objective": "Cultivate critical thinking, deepen understanding of the translational value of TME research"
    },
    {
      "activity_name": "Exit Ticket Assessment",
      "duration": "20 minutes",
      "procedure": "Students are required to write two parts on the exit ticket: (1) One core mechanism by which TME promotes cancer progression; (2) One confusing question you still have after this class. Instructor collects all tickets for learning effect assessment.",
      "activity_objective": "Check students' mastery of core knowledge, identify unsolved problems for follow-up explanation"
    }
  ],
  "after_class_assignment": [
    "Read a recent short review on TME heterogeneity and immunotherapy resistance (instructor provides the article link)",
    "Write a 200-300 word reflection: How can we use our understanding of TME to improve the efficacy of existing cancer therapies?"
  ],
  "teaching_support_materials": [
    "PowerPoint slides with TME structure diagrams and clinical case data",
    "Blank concept map templates for group activities",
    "Recommended review articles and recent clinical trial updates on TME-targeted therapies"
  ]
}

{
  "course_basic_info": {
