      "Analyze real clinical cases to adjust drug regimens based on pharmacogenomic data",
      "Identify the limitations and challenges of current pharmacogenomic applications"
    ],
    "quality_objectives": [
      "Establish the concept of patient-centered precision medicine",
      "Recognize ethical issues related to genetic data privacy and genetic discrimination",
      "Stimulate interest in translational research of personalized medicine"
    ]
  },
  "key_points": [
    "Core concept of pharmacogenomics and its core value in personalized medicine",
    "Typical pharmacogenomic biomarkers and their clinical significance",
    "Current clinical application scenarios of pharmacogenomics"
  ],
  "difficulties": [
    "Mechanistic connection between genetic variation and interindividual drug response variability",
    "Integrating pharmacogenomic results into clinical treatment decision-making",
    "Critical evaluation of the benefits, limitations and ethical challenges of pharmacogenomic testing"
  ],
  "classroom_activity_design": {
    "activity_type": "Case-based group discussion",
    "activity_objective": "Bridge theoretical knowledge and clinical practice, train students' ability to solve real-world problems with pharmacogenomic knowledge",
    "pre_class_preparation": [
      "Students form 4-person groups in advance 1 week before class",
      "Assign pre-reading: Excerpt of Clinical Pharmacogenetics Implementation Consortium (CPIC) guidelines for warfarin and 5-fluorouracil",
      "Watch pre-recorded 8-minute introductory video on basic concepts of genetic variation"
    ],
    "in_class_procedure": [
      {
        "step": "Step 1: Case distribution (1 minute)",
        "content": "Each group gets one of two clinical cases: \nCase A (Warfarin Dosing): 58-year-old male with deep vein thrombosis, needs warfarin initiation. Genotyping result: VKORC1 c.-1639G>A: AA genotype, CYP2C9: *1/*3. Questions: 1. What is his expected dose requirement compared to an average patient? 2. What adjustment will you recommend, and what adverse reaction are you avoiding?\nCase B (5-FU Chemotherapy): 38-year-old female with colorectal cancer, planned for capecitabine (5-FU prodrug) chemotherapy. Genotyping shows DPYD *2A/*1 (heterozygous loss-of-function variant). Questions: 1. What is the risk of standard dose capecitabine? 2. What treatment adjustment will you recommend?"
      },
      {
        "step": "Step 2: Group discussion (10 minutes)",
        "content": "Groups discuss the questions with a hint worksheet. Instructor patrols to answer questions and guide thinking."
