    "Maps of European territorial changes before and after the treaty",
    "1-minute multimedia clip of the 1919 signing ceremony"
  ],
  "classroom_activity_design": [
    {
      "stage": "Lead-in (5 minutes)",
      "content": "Show two contrasting images: destroyed WWI Western Front trenches, and Allied leaders signing the treaty at Versailles. Ask the hook question: \"After 4 years of devastating war, the whole world craved peace. But why did the 1919 peace at Versailles only lead to a bigger world war 20 years later? Today we will explore the causes and consequences of this influential treaty.\""
    },
    {
      "stage": "Activity 1: Role-play to explore causes (15 minutes)",
      "content": "1. Divide students into 3 groups, each representing one of the Big Three Allied leaders: Clemenceau (France), Lloyd George (Britain), Wilson (US). Each group gets a role card with core national interests: France seeks revenge, maximum reparations, and long-term security against Germany; Britain wants to balance European power, gain German colonies, and avoid completely crushing Germany; the US promotes idealistic open diplomacy, a League of Nations, and national self-determination. 2. Groups discuss for 3 minutes to sort out non-negotiable demands, then each sends a representative to present their position. 3. Teacher summarizes the causes: After the Allied victory in WWI 1918, a formal peace settlement was required. Competing interests of the Big Three, plus the exclusion of Germany and Soviet Russia from negotiations, led to the unfair compromise that became the final treaty."
    },
    {
      "stage": "Activity 2: Jigsaw analysis to sort out consequences (20 minutes)",
      "content": "1. Expert group stage: Divide students into 4 expert groups, each responsible for one dimension of consequences: Group 1 (Consequences for Germany), Group 2 (Consequences for Allied powers), Group 3 (Consequences for Eastern Europe and colonies), Group 4 (Long-term consequences for global peace). Groups discuss primary sources for 5 minutes to sort out core findings. 2. Jigsaw share stage: Students reorganize into mixed groups with one expert from each dimension, and each expert shares their findings, so all students master all dimensions. 3. Whole class discussion: Teacher raises the question \"Was the Treaty of Versailles too harsh on Germany, or too weak?\" Invite 3-4 students to share their views. 4. Teacher summarizes consequences: Immediate: Ended the state of war, dissolved four old empires (German, Austro-Hungarian, Ottoman, Russian), created new independent nation states in Eastern Europe. Long-term negative: Harsh terms (war guilt clause, heavy reparations, territorial losses) sparked extreme nationalist resentment in Germany exploited by the Nazis; the League of Nations was weakened by the US non-participation and exclusion of major powers; new minority conflicts were created in Eastern Europe, and colonial self-determination was ignored. Ultimately, the treaty became a key root cause of World War II."
    },
    {
      "stage": "Summary and Assignment (5 minutes)",
      "content": "1. Summary: Recap that the Treaty of Versailles was shaped by winner-takes-all logic after WWI, and its unfair settlement created more problems than it solved. 2. Assignment: Write a 300-word reflection: If you were a peace negotiator in 1919, what changes would you make to the treaty to prevent future war?"
    }
  ],
  "blackboard_design": {
