  "step_by_step_approach": [
    {
      "step_number": 1,
      "title": "Recall core definitions and theorems",
      "content": "A function is analytic at all points in the complex plane (called an entire function) if it is complex-differentiable at every z in the complex plane ℂ. Key results: (1) Sums, products, and compositions of analytic functions are analytic on their domains; (2) All polynomials on ℂ are entire, and the complex exponential function w ↦ e^w is also entire."
    },
    {
      "step_number": 2,
      "title": "Proof via properties of analytic functions",
      "content": "Decompose f(z) as the composition of two entire functions: g(z) = z^2 (a polynomial, hence entire) and h(w) = e^w (an entire function). Then f(z) = h(g(z)), the composition of two entire functions. By the composition property of analytic functions, f(z) is analytic at all points in ℂ."
    },
    {
      "step_number": 3,
      "title": "Alternative direct verification via Cauchy-Riemann equations",
      "content": "Separate f(z) into real and imaginary parts: Let z = x + iy, so z^2 = (x^2 - y^2) + i(2xy). By Euler's formula, f(z) = e^(x^2 - y^2)cos(2xy) + i e^(x^2 - y^2)sin(2xy). Thus we get real part u(x,y) = e^(x^2 - y^2)cos(2xy) and imaginary part v(x,y) = e^(x^2 - y^2)sin(2xy)."
    },
    {
      "step_number": 4,
      "title": "Check continuity and Cauchy-Riemann conditions",
      "content": "First, all first-order partial derivatives of u and v are combinations of smooth functions (exponentials, polynomials, trigonometric functions), so they are continuous everywhere on ℝ². Next verify the Cauchy-Riemann conditions that hold for all analytic functions: (1) ∂u/∂x = 2e^(x² - y²)(x cos2xy - y sin2xy) = ∂v/∂y; (2) ∂u/∂y = -2e^(x² - y²)(y cos2xy + x sin2xy) = -∂v/∂x. Both conditions hold for all (x,y)."
    },
    {
      "step_number": 5,
      "title": "Draw conclusion from Cauchy-Riemann criterion",
      "content": "If a function has continuous partial derivatives that satisfy Cauchy-Riemann equations everywhere, it is complex-differentiable at all z ∈ ℂ, hence analytic everywhere."
    }
  ],
  "final_result": "f(z) = e^(z^2) is analytic at all points in the complex plane."
}

