  "approach_overview": "A trie (prefix tree) is a tree-structured data structure optimized for storing words with shared prefixes, where each node represents a single character. Shared prefixes reuse nodes, leading to efficient storage and fast prefix-based search operations. The implementation uses two core components: a `TrieNode` class to represent individual nodes, and a `Trie` class to manage the structure and core operations (insert, full word search, prefix search).",
  "step_by_step_implementation": [
    "1. Define the TrieNode structure: Each node has two core properties: (a) a dictionary (hash map) `children` that maps character keys to child TrieNode objects, enabling O(1) character lookups; (b) a boolean `is_end_of_word` flag to mark if the node represents the end of a complete stored word.",
    "2. Initialize the Trie: The root of the trie is an empty TrieNode, with no preceding characters.",
    "3. Implement insert operation: Start at the root node. Iterate over each character in the input word: If the character does not exist in the current node's children, create a new TrieNode for the character and add it to the children dictionary. Move the current pointer to the child node. After processing all characters, set the `is_end_of_word` flag of the final node to `True` to mark the end of the full word.",
    "4. Implement full word search: Start at the root. Iterate over each character in the target word. If any character is not found in the current node's children, return `False` immediately (word does not exist). After processing all characters, return the value of `is_end_of_word` to confirm the sequence is a complete stored word (not just a prefix).",
    "5. Implement prefix search: Follow the same traversal as full word search. If all characters of the prefix are found during traversal, return `True` (no check for `is_end_of_word` is needed, as we only confirm the prefix exists in the trie)."
