      "step_description": "Relate decision tree height to number of leaves. Let h be the height of the decision tree, which equals the maximum number of comparisons (i.e., worst-case running time) the algorithm makes for any input of size n. A binary tree of height h can have at most 2^h leaf nodes. Combining with the requirement from step 1, we get the core inequality: 2^h ≥ n!"
    },
    {
      "step_number": 3,
      "step_description": "Derive the lower bound for h. Take base-2 logarithm of both sides of the inequality to get h ≥ log₂(n!). We then lower bound log₂(n!): for n ≥ 2, n! = n × (n-1) × ... × 1 ≥ (n/2)^(n/2) (the n/2 largest terms in the product are all at least n/2). Substituting this gives log₂(n!) ≥ log₂((n/2)^(n/2)) = (n/2)log₂(n/2) = Ω(n log n)."
    },
    {
      "step_number": 4,
      "step_description": "Conclude the lower bound. The height h of the decision tree is the minimum possible worst-case number of comparisons for any correct comparison-based sorting algorithm. Since h is lower bounded by Ω(n log n), any comparison-based sorting algorithm has a lower bound of Ω(n log n) for sorting n distinct elements."
    }
  ],
  "core_intuition": "This is an information-theoretic argument: sorting n distinct elements requires distinguishing between n! possible input permutations, each comparison gives at most 1 bit of information, so we need at least log₂(n!) = Ω(n log n) comparisons to correctly sort any input."
}

{
  "cap_core_definitions": {
    "consistency": "All client reads return the most recent write from any node; every client sees identical data regardless of which node they connect to at any point in time",
    "availability": "Every client request receives a successful non-error response (even if the response does not reflect the latest update)",
    "partition tolerance": "The system continues to operate despite arbitrary network partitions (splits of the distributed cluster into disconnected subsets of nodes that cannot communicate with each other) caused by network outages or packet loss"
  },
  "explanation_why_all_three_cannot_coexist": "Network partitions are an inevitable, unavoidable failure mode for any practical distributed system (networks are inherently unreliable, so partitions will occur at some point). This means partition tolerance is a required property for any distributed system intended to run reliably, so the system must make an unavoidable tradeoff between consistency and availability when a partition occurs: If the system chooses to maintain consistency, it must block writes to any disconnected partitions to prevent divergent data, which means some client requests will fail, violating availability. If the system chooses to maintain availability, it allows writes to all disconnected partitions, which leads to divergent, conflicting data across partitions, violating consistency. There is no way to retain both properties during a partition, so all three CAP properties cannot be simultaneously guaranteed.",
  "practical_scenario_prioritizing_availability_over_consistency": "A global social media platform handling like counters for a viral public post that receives millions of interactions per minute from users across the world. The post and its like counter are replicated across 15 regional data centers to keep latency low for local users.",
  "implications_of_prioritizing_availability": [
    "Positive outcome: No user is blocked from interacting with the post even during an inter-data-center network partition. Every user gets a successful response when they try to like the post, which preserves a smooth user experience, a critical business priority for consumer social media.",
    "Temporary inconsistency: Users in different disconnected regions will see slightly different like counter values during the partition. For example, a user in North America may see 1.24 million likes while a user in Southeast Asia sees 1.238 million likes, because new interactions from each region cannot sync across the split network.",
    "Eventual consistency: Once the network partition heals, the system can easily reconcile divergent counts (like increments are commutative and idempotent, so merging just requires summing all independent increments), so the count will converge to a consistent correct value across all nodes.",
    "Acceptable tradeoff: Temporary minor inconsistency has no meaningful business or user impact in this scenario, so the gain of 100% availability far outweighs the cost of temporary inconsistency.",
    "Minor implementation overhead: The system requires lightweight post-partition conflict resolution logic, which adds minimal development complexity compared to the benefits of higher availability."
  ]
}

