{
  "nash_equilibrium_core_definition": "In a non-cooperative game, a Nash Equilibrium is a stable combination of strategies (one strategy chosen by each independent player) where no individual player can improve their own payoff (outcome reward) by changing their strategy unilaterally, if all other players keep their strategies unchanged.",
  "key_non_cooperative_game_context": "Non-cooperative games are defined by the absence of enforceable, binding pre-agreements between players. Players act independently to pursue their own individual self-interest, and cannot coordinate to enforce a collectively better outcome that goes against an individual's self-interest. This makes Nash Equilibrium the standard solution concept for predicting stable outcomes in these games.",
  "core_stability_property": "A Nash Equilibrium is a self-reinforcing steady state: no player has any incentive to deviate from their current strategy, because any unilateral deviation would leave them worse off (or no better off) than their outcome in the equilibrium.",
  "classic_concrete_example_prisoners_dilemma": {
    "game_setup": "Two suspects are arrested for a crime, interrogated separately with no ability to communicate or make binding deals (making this a non-cooperative game). Each can choose to 'Confess' or 'Stay Silent'. The payoffs (prison sentences) are: 1) Both confess: 5 years each; 2) Both stay silent: 1 year each; 3) One confesses, one stays silent: confessor is freed (0 years), the silent suspect gets 10 years.",
    "nash_equilibrium_outcome": "(Confess, Confess) is the only pure-strategy Nash Equilibrium here. If the other player stays on 'Confess', neither player can improve their outcome by switching to 'Stay Silent': switching would increase their sentence from 5 years to 10 years. No player has an incentive to deviate unilaterally, so this combination is a stable equilibrium.",
    "key_observation": "This example demonstrates that a Nash Equilibrium does not need to be socially or collectively optimal. While both staying silent gives a better outcome for both players, that combination is not a Nash Equilibrium because each player has an individual incentive to deviate (cheat on the unenforceable agreement to stay silent) to get a better outcome for themselves."
  },
  "additional_key_notes": "A single non-cooperative game can have multiple distinct Nash Equilibria. While some games have no Nash Equilibrium in pure strategies (fixed single choices), Nash's theorem proves every finite non-cooperative game has at least one Nash Equilibrium when mixed strategies (probability-weighted combinations of pure strategies) are allowed."
}

