      "key_points": [
        "Recap that the Arrow-Debreu existence theorem proves at least one Walrasian equilibrium (market-clearing price vector) exists under standard assumptions: continuous, convex, monotonic consumer preferences; convex firm production sets; complete markets; no externalities.",
        "Note that these assumptions only guarantee existence via fixed-point theorems (Brouwer/Kakutani), relying only on two properties of aggregate excess demand: continuity and satisfaction of Walras' law.",
        "Highlight that these two properties are fully compatible with multiple equilibria and unstable equilibria; no additional restrictions for uniqueness/stability are required to prove existence."
      ]
    },
    {
      "step_id": 2,
      "step_name": "Explain Why Existence Does Not Imply Uniqueness",
      "step_description": "Use core Arrow-Debreu general equilibrium results to demonstrate that multiple equilibria are consistent with the existence conditions.",
      "key_points": [
        "Introduce the Sonnenschein-Mantel-Debreu (SMD) theorem, a central result for the Arrow-Debreu framework: it proves that any continuous function satisfying Walras' law can be generated as the aggregate excess demand function of a well-defined Arrow-Debreu economy that meets all standard existence assumptions.",
        "Explain that this means aggregate excess demand can cross the zero (market-clearing) line multiple times at different price vectors, even when all existence conditions hold. Income effects across heterogeneous consumers regularly generate this shape without violating any existence assumptions.",
        "Clarify that uniqueness of Walrasian equilibrium only holds under strong extra assumptions (e.g. global gross substitutability of all goods) that are not required for existence, so existence never guarantees uniqueness."
      ]
    },
    {
      "step_id": 3,
      "step_name": "Explain Why Existence Does Not Imply Stability",
      "step_description": "Clarify that stability is a dynamic property of price adjustment processes, which is not addressed by the static existence result.",
      "key_points": [
        "Define stability (local stability: convergence to equilibrium from any nearby price vector; global stability: convergence from any initial price) for the standard tatonnement adjustment process commonly used to test equilibrium stability in general equilibrium.",
        "Again use the SMD theorem to show that aggregate excess demand can take any shape satisfying Walras' law: around an existing equilibrium, excess demand can be upward-sloping rather than downward-sloping. If price deviates slightly upward, positive excess demand pushes price even further away from equilibrium, making the existing equilibrium unstable.",
        "Note that the Arrow-Debreu existence result is purely static: it only confirms that a market-clearing price vector exists, it makes no claims about how the economy adjusts to reach that vector. Even when multiple equilibria exist, some are stable and others are unstable, so existence of an equilibrium never guarantees it is stable.",
        "Add that global stability only holds under the same strong extra assumptions (e.g. global gross substitutes) that are not required for existence in the Arrow-Debreu model."
      ]
    },
    {
      "step_id": 4,
      "step_name": "Concluding Synthesis",
      "step_description": "Summarize the separation of the three properties in Arrow-Debreu general equilibrium.",
      "key_points": [
        "Existence, uniqueness and stability are distinct properties: existence only requires weak restrictions on the primitives of the Arrow-Debreu economy, while uniqueness and stability require much stronger additional restrictions that are not implied by existence conditions.",
        "The Sonnenschein-Mantel-Debreu theorem is the core theoretical result that formalizes why existence does not guarantee uniqueness or stability in general equilibrium theory."
