  "core_premise": "Agency theory frames the relationship as a contract between self-interested parties, where principals (shareholders) delegate decision-making authority to agents (managers), so the approach builds from this foundation to map conflicts, costs, and alignment mechanisms",
  "approach_steps": [
    {
      "step_number": 1,
      "step_name": "Anchor the core agency relationship to public company context",
      "step_details": "First formalize the defining characteristics of the relationship: 1) Confirm principals = shareholders (residual owners of the firm, goal of maximizing long-term shareholder value, do not manage daily operations); 2) Confirm agents = executive managers (hold operational/strategic control, compensated for their decision-making); 3) Highlight the separation of ownership and control (the defining feature of public companies that creates the agency relationship that agency theory is designed to explain)."
    },
    {
      "step_number": 2,
      "step_name": "Map sources of principal-agent conflict per agency theory assumptions",
      "step_details": "Apply the core assumption of self-interested behavior to identify specific conflicts: 1) Divergent risk preferences: shareholders can diversify their portfolios and accept profitable risky projects, while managers avoid risk to protect their job security and human capital tied to the firm; 2) Divergent time horizons: managers prioritize short-term performance metrics (quarterly EPS) to trigger bonuses or promotions, while shareholders focus on long-term cash flow growth; 3) Private benefit extraction: managers divert firm resources to excessive perquisites (private jets, luxury offices) or inflated compensation that does not benefit shareholders; 4) Empire building: managers pursue value-destroying acquisitions/overexpansion to increase their own power and pay, even if it reduces shareholder value."
    },
    {
      "step_number": 3,
      "step_name": "Categorize agency costs (core output of agency theory)",
      "step_details": "Break down the total costs of misalignment as defined by agency theory: 1) Monitoring costs: costs shareholders incur to oversee managers (audit fees, board governance costs, regulatory compliance, shareholder activism expenses); 2) Bonding costs: costs managers incur to credibly commit to acting in shareholder interests (performance-based contracting, non-compete agreements); 3) Residual loss: value lost from misaligned decisions that persists even after monitoring and bonding (e.g. value destroyed by an unblocked bad acquisition)."
    },
    {
      "step_number": 4,
      "step_name": "Evaluate alignment mechanisms prescribed by agency theory",
      "step_details": "Assess how governance mechanisms reduce conflict: 1) Incentive alignment: equity-based compensation (restricted stock, stock options) that ties manager wealth to shareholder value; 2) Internal governance: independent boards and audit committees that act as fiduciaries for shareholders; 3) External governance: the threat of hostile takeovers that disciplines underperforming managers; 4) Institutional shareholder activism: large investors intervening to correct misaligned behavior; 5) Regulation: rules like Sarbanes-Oxley that reduce information asymmetry between managers and shareholders."
    },
    {
      "step_number": 5,
      "step_name": "Test agency theory against real-world outcomes",
      "step_details": "Evaluate how well the framework explains modern public company dynamics, such as excessive CEO pay, short-termism from share buybacks, or management entrenchment via anti-takeover provisions, to confirm the relevance of agency theory to the relationship."
    }
  ],
  "final_synthesis_focus": "Conclude by summarizing that agency theory frames the shareholder-manager relationship as an inevitable conflict of interest from separated ownership and control, which can be mitigated but never fully eliminated by governance mechanisms, resulting in persistent residual agency costs for all public companies."
