{
  "core_traditional_sociological_understanding_of_reality": "Early and traditional sociology (most notably positivist and structural-functionalist traditions) frames social reality as an objective, fixed, naturally occurring phenomenon that exists independently of human perception and social interaction. It follows Emile Durkheim's framing of 'social facts' as external, constraining, measurable entities that can be studied like natural phenomena, assuming core social categories and structures have inherent, unchanging properties.",
