{
  "concept_of_cognitive_dissonance": "Cognitive dissonance is a foundational social psychological concept first proposed by Leon Festinger, referring to the uncomfortable psychological tension that arises when an individual holds two or more conflicting cognitions — these can be clashing beliefs, values, attitudes, or a mismatch between a person's core beliefs and their actual behavior. Humans have an innate drive to maintain cognitive consistency, so when dissonance occurs, people are motivated to reduce the discomfort through adjustments like changing one conflicting cognition, adding a new justifying belief, or downplaying the importance of the inconsistency.",
  "example_of_cognitive_dissonance": "A common everyday example of cognitive dissonance is experienced by someone who believes strongly in protecting the environment and reducing carbon emissions, but regularly drives a large gas-powered truck for daily commuting because they enjoy the vehicle. The two conflicting cognitions here are: 1) 'I need to reduce my carbon footprint to help stop climate change' and 2) 'I drive a high-emission truck every day'. This mismatch creates cognitive dissonance. To resolve the discomfort, the individual might justify their behavior by adding a new cognition like 'I recycle and buy organic food, so that makes up for my truck's emissions', or downplay the conflict by saying 'one person's driving doesn't make any difference anyway', or eventually change their behavior by switching to a more fuel-efficient vehicle to align their actions with their beliefs."
}

