{
  "core_theoretical_premise": "First formalized by Leon Festinger in 1957, cognitive dissonance theory starts from the assumption that humans have an innate psychological need to maintain consistency among their beliefs, attitudes, values, self-perceptions, and actions. When an individual holds conflicting beliefs or acts in a way that contradicts their existing beliefs, this creates an aversive, uncomfortable state of tension called cognitive dissonance. This discomfort acts as a motivational force that pushes people to resolve the inconsistency, often resulting in behavior change.",
